        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people pick a surface cleaner by looking at their driveway and buying the biggest deck they can afford. That is backwards. A surface cleaner's bar is spun by your washer's water flow, and a deck too large for that flow turns slowly and leaves swirls. Size the deck to your machine's GPM first. A well-matched 15-inch deck will clean a driveway better than a starved 20-inch one, even if it takes a little longer.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Your Washer's Flow Decides the Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner has no motor. Its spray bar spins because the nozzles are angled, and the water leaving them pushes the bar around like a lawn sprinkler. The more water flows through the nozzles, the faster and more forcefully the bar turns. That is why gallons per minute, not pounds per square inch, is the first number to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger decks have longer bars. A longer bar has more mass and more drag, and its nozzles sit further from the centre, so it needs more flow to reach the same speed. Put a 20-inch deck on a washer that only supplies 2 GPM and the bar crawls round. A slow bar does not sweep each spot enough times, which leaves the arcs and swirls covered in our guide to <a href="/surface-cleaner-leaving-streaks/">surface cleaner streaks</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure still matters, but in a different way. PSI determines how hard each jet hits the surface, and it falls as water spreads across more nozzles or a bigger orifice. A deck with nozzles too large for your washer drops the working pressure, and the cleaning effect drops with it. Our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-psi-vs-gpm/">PSI vs GPM</a> covers the trade-off in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the right size is the largest deck your washer can spin at full speed with enough pressure at each nozzle. Anything larger cleans worse, not faster. Anything much smaller works but takes longer than it needs to.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size to GPM first, then confirm PSI.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Find your washer's rated flow, pick the deck class that matches it, then check that the deck's pressure rating covers your machine.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="numbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Read Your Washer's Numbers Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Find the rated PSI and GPM on the washer's label or in its manual. Gas washers commonly list figures around 2,800 to 4,000 PSI and 2.3 to 4.0 GPM. Electric washers typically sit around 1,500 to 2,000 PSI and 1.2 to 1.8 GPM, though a few higher-output electric models go further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be aware that headline numbers are often maximum ratings. Some manufacturers quote peak pressure that the pump only reaches briefly, and the sustained working figures are lower. When a spec sheet lists both a maximum and a rated figure, use the rated one for sizing. If you are not sure, size down rather than up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your water supply also sets a ceiling. A washer rated for 4 GPM cannot deliver it if your spigot, hose or well pump supplies less. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/">pressure washer water supply requirements</a> explains how to check the flow at your tap with a bucket and a timer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the fitting while you are at it. Most universal decks use a 1/4-inch quick-connect plug, but some brand attachments use a bayonet or proprietary coupler. A deck that is the right size but the wrong connector needs an adapter, which is fine, but worth knowing before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick bucket test tells you what your setup really delivers. With the washer running and the gun open, time how long it takes to fill a five-gallon bucket from the wand without a nozzle, or check the flow at the spigot before the washer. If the result is well below the label, the deck you choose should reflect the real number, not the advertised one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Surface Cleaner Sizing Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below is a rule of thumb, not a guarantee. Individual decks vary by nozzle configuration and bar design, and the manufacturer's stated range for a specific model always takes priority. Treat these figures as typical ranges that match what most manufacturers list.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Deck Diameter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Washer Output</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nozzles</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 to 14 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 to 2,000 PSI, 1.5 to 2.0 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric washers, patios, walks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">15 to 16 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,800 to 3,400 PSI, 2.3 to 3.0 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dual or quad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most homeowner gas washers, driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">17 to 18 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,200 to 4,000 PSI, 3.0 to 4.0 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large gas washers, bigger driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 in and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,600+ PSI, 4.0+ GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commercial gas washers, large lots</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most homeowners land in the 15 to 16 inch class. It covers the typical gas washer sold for home use and handles a two-car driveway comfortably. If your washer sits at the low end of a range, pick the smaller deck in that class.
          </p>

          <p>
            The jump to 20 inches and above only makes sense with a genuinely high-flow washer, typically a commercial or prosumer gas unit with 4 GPM or more. On anything less, a large deck is a step backwards. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner rankings</a> list the recommended washer class for each attachment we cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you sit between two classes, think about how you use the washer. Mostly patios and walks with occasional driveway work points to the smaller deck. Mostly large driveways with a strong gas machine points to the bigger one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nozzles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Dual-Nozzle vs Quad-Nozzle Decks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dual-nozzle bars use two opposing jets. They are simple, easy to balance and forgiving across a wide range of washers, which is why most 12 to 16 inch decks use them. With only two nozzles sharing the flow, each jet gets more of the available water, so they work well on modest machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quad-nozzle bars use four jets. Because there are more jets, each spot gets swept more often per revolution, which tends to give a more even finish on textured or stamped concrete and allows a slightly faster walking pace. The trade-off is that the flow is split four ways, so each nozzle needs a smaller orifice and the washer needs more total GPM to keep pressure up.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a high-flow gas washer, a quad-nozzle deck is usually the better choice in the 16 to 20 inch range. On a lower-flow washer, a quad-nozzle deck often underperforms a well-matched dual-nozzle deck of the same size, because each jet ends up too weak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nozzle orifice size matters as much as nozzle count. Many decks let you swap nozzles, and manufacturers often publish a chart matching orifice size to washer PSI and GPM. If your deck's nozzles were sized for a bigger machine than yours, changing them can make a mismatched deck work properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bar balance is the other reason nozzle choice matters. A bar with one nozzle partly blocked, or with mismatched nozzles after a careless replacement, wobbles as it spins. That wobble wears the swivel faster and shows up on the slab as an uneven pattern. Buying replacement nozzles in matched sets avoids it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing for an Electric Pressure Washer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric washers are where sizing mistakes are most common. Their flow is typically well under 2 GPM, so most large aftermarket decks designed for gas machines will spin too slowly. A 12 to 14 inch deck, or one specifically marketed as compatible with electric washers, is the realistic choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand-matched attachments are often the safest bet for electric machines, because they are tuned to that brand's flow. The trade-off is a proprietary connector that may not fit other washers. Universal decks with smaller nozzles can work too, if the manufacturer lists your washer's flow range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect a slower job. A small deck on an electric washer covers less area per pass and needs a slower walking pace. On a two-car driveway, that is the difference between a morning and a long afternoon. It still beats a wand for evenness, though. Our guide to whether an <a href="/can-an-electric-pressure-washer-clean-concrete/">electric pressure washer can clean concrete</a> covers what to expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you regularly clean large areas, the deck is not the limit; the washer is. At that point a higher-flow gas machine does more for the result than any attachment. Our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer rankings</a> list rated flow for each machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose length matters more on electric machines than on gas ones. Their pumps have less margin, so a long, narrow supply hose that barely affects a gas washer can drop an electric washer's flow enough to slow the bar. Use the shortest practical supply hose and a full-bore spigot when running a surface cleaner on an electric unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="area" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Area, Time and Why Bigger Is Not Always Faster
          </h2>

          <p>
            A larger deck covers more ground per pass in theory. A 20-inch deck sweeps a lane about a third wider than a 15-inch deck, so on paper it should finish a driveway a third faster. That only holds if the bar spins at full speed and you can keep the same walking pace.
          </p>

          <p>
            On an under-powered washer, a big deck forces you to slow down to avoid swirls, which eats the time saving. You can end up taking just as long with a worse finish. A matched deck at its proper pace is usually the fastest route to a clean, even slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight and handling also change with size. Large decks are heavier and harder to steer around obstacles, and they cannot reach into corners or along narrow walks. Many contractors own two decks: a large one for open flatwork and a small one for walks, steps and patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a typical homeowner with a single gas washer, one well-matched 15 or 16 inch deck plus the wand for corners covers almost everything. Our <a href="/how-to-use-a-pressure-washer-surface-cleaner/">surface cleaner technique guide</a> explains pace and overlap for whichever size you choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough sense of scale, a typical two-car driveway is somewhere around 400 to 600 square feet. At a careful pace with good overlap, a matched 15 to 16 inch deck usually covers that in an hour or two of actual cleaning time, depending on how dirty it is and how much pre-treating it needs. Treat that as a ballpark, not a promise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Choose, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with your washer's rated GPM and PSI. Find the deck class in the table that matches the flow, then confirm the deck's pressure rating covers your machine's maximum so you do not over-pressurise the swivel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the nozzle configuration. On a lower-flow washer, prefer a dual-nozzle deck. On a high-flow gas washer in the larger sizes, a quad-nozzle deck usually gives a more even finish. Confirm the nozzles are replaceable and that the maker lists sizes for your machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm the connector fits your wand or gun, and budget for an adapter if not. Look for a sealed or serviceable swivel, sturdy casters and a full brush skirt, since those are the parts that wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the surfaces you clean most. Large open driveways favour the biggest deck your washer can spin well. Patios, walks and pavers favour a smaller, more manoeuvrable deck. If you are cleaning a patio, our guide to <a href="/how-to-pressure-wash-a-patio/">pressure washing a patio</a> covers material-specific settings. For stubborn spots the deck cannot shift, a <a href="/surface-cleaner-vs-turbo-nozzle/">turbo nozzle</a> is the usual partner tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a deck and it seems mismatched, try new nozzles before buying a different attachment. A set of correctly sized nozzles costs little and can turn a sluggish deck into one that spins at its intended speed on your washer. Only replace the whole deck if the manufacturer offers no nozzle sizes for your flow range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the biggest deck for a big driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driveway size only affects how long the job takes. Your washer's flow decides whether the deck spins fast enough to clean evenly. A large deck on a modest washer spins slowly and leaves swirls, which costs more time than it saves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing from peak PSI instead of rated GPM</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Peak pressure figures are often brief maximums. Flow is what spins the bar, and rated figures are what the pump sustains. Size from rated GPM, then confirm the deck's pressure rating covers your machine's maximum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a quad-nozzle deck on a low-flow washer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Four nozzles split the flow four ways. On a washer that cannot supply enough GPM, each jet is weak and the finish suffers. A dual-nozzle deck of the same size often cleans better on smaller machines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the water supply</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A washer rated for 4 GPM delivers less if the spigot or hose cannot supply it. Measure your tap's flow with a bucket and timer before buying a large deck that depends on full output.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the connector</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Universal decks usually use a 1/4-inch quick-connect, while some brand attachments use bayonet or proprietary fittings. Check what your wand accepts, and budget for an adapter if needed, before the deck arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size surface cleaner do I need for a 3,000 PSI pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most 3,000 PSI gas washers sold for home use deliver roughly 2.3 to 2.8 GPM, which typically suits a 15 to 16 inch deck. Check your washer's rated GPM, since flow matters more than pressure for spinning the bar, and confirm the deck's pressure rating covers your machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size surface cleaner works with an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually a 12 to 14 inch deck, or one sold specifically as electric-compatible. Electric washers typically supply under 2 GPM, which is not enough to spin most large decks designed for gas machines. A matched small deck cleans more evenly than a starved big one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 20 inch surface cleaner better than a 15 inch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on a washer with enough flow to spin it, typically 4 GPM or more. On that kind of machine, a 20 inch deck covers ground faster. On a typical homeowner gas washer, a 15 or 16 inch deck usually cleans more evenly and finishes just as quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does surface cleaner size depend on PSI or GPM?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainly GPM. The bar is spun by water flowing through its nozzles, so flow decides how fast and evenly it turns. PSI decides how hard each jet hits. Size the deck to your washer's rated GPM first, then confirm its pressure rating covers your machine's maximum PSI.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are quad-nozzle surface cleaners better than dual-nozzle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On high-flow gas washers, quad-nozzle decks often give a more even finish, especially on textured concrete. On lower-flow washers, the flow is split too thinly across four jets and a dual-nozzle deck usually performs better. Match the nozzle count to your washer's output.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I change the nozzles on my surface cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On many decks, yes. Manufacturers often publish a chart matching nozzle orifice size to washer PSI and GPM. Swapping to the correct nozzles can make a slightly mismatched deck spin and clean properly. Always replace nozzles as a matched set so the bar stays balanced.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size surface cleaner is best for a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Patios usually favour a smaller deck in the 12 to 16 inch range. It is easier to steer around furniture and planters and reaches closer to edges. If the patio is pavers or natural stone, a lower pressure and careful passes matter more than deck size.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

